A||N||7||RobertVol||Manifold Question||||5||
Z||000000||RobertVol||05-13-2001||09:34 PM||||I have seen some V-6 Exaust Manifolds with Gaskets and some without...PLease explain.<P>I would like to know why some do and some dont. I have a small leak around the .....<P>(gulp) rear one. But the leak is small and seems to be coming from the Left side near the mounting of the manifold right at the block. Since its the pain in the rear one, is this the time to just break down and get some Sprint Manifolds or Chris Wests Headers? <P>This is not a question of which one is better between Sprints and Chris Wests<P>Just should I try to fix the Leak and replace the manifold or is this the time to upgrade? <P>Opinions? <P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P>CLICK BELOW AND SEE OUR SITE !!! <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=819451&a=5959473&p=36934984&Sequence=1"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||bHooper||05-13-2001||10:00 PM||||Robert,<P>I had leaky manifolds on my GT and through much deliberation, and consultation with those here as well as Ed Parks, I decided to replace them. <P>I used Permatex Ultra Copper liquid gasket stuff (apparently, this is all Ed use's) on both the front and rear.  I also used the saturn manifold studs and flange nuts (Ed). <P>After close to 3 months and a couple thousand miles, there are no exhaust leaks (although it appears i have a crack forming in one of the replacement manifolds).  It should also be noted that i only have 5 studs holding on the front manifold, as there was a broken studd in the head.  I made sure to use some extra permatex in this area and it has held up nicely!  Oh, yeah, I also applied the permatex onto the heat shield (as well as the manifold) on the rear, so there would be no chance of a leak.  The recall only included one gasket (between the manifold and the shield)!<P>As for working on the rear manifold, the easiest way to do it is by removing the rear cradle bolts, deck lid, and swinging the a/c compressor out of the way.  This allows you to litterally tilt the engine back towards the trunk, so that you can lay on top of it and replacethe manifold.<P>If you do a search, you will find my postings where I have listed the Saturn parts numbers.  There are two for the studs, but only one of them are good.<P>Best of luck, and feel free to email me if you have any questions!<P>hoop ||||reg||6
Z||000002||RobertVol||05-13-2001||10:33 PM||||Thanks for the info hoop!<P>I know I may be in for alot of headaches<P>I had  saw that post you had about the bolts from saturn. good info. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks,<P><P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P>CLICK BELOW AND SEE OUR SITE !!! <A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=819451&a=5959473&p=36934984&Sequence=1"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cozmo||05-14-2001||08:07 AM||||Just a thumbs up bhooper!<BR>I got the Saturn studs and nuts from my local dealer. The parts dept. was great, they didn't have them in stock but got them in next day no extra charge.  The total was $27, I got 12 studs and 20 nuts (2 bags of 10). Using the permatex stuff and antiseize compound, if I ever have to take them off again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> it should be easy.<BR>C02M0||||reg||14
